522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1045 "Adding and Subtracting Radical Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179899 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "847ae41b-99e9-5c25-94db-a091578bf000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180522 #edb/ref 1061 36452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e6999c1-79fe-5006-91df-3c25468791e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180523 #edb/ref 1061 36453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "145c40ab-6bce-539c-acfa-6819834eff82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1061 36694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e61e6895-553c-5528-9f47-4ce31a87b2fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1061 36695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ed96a02-bacb-559e-9bf4-ebf7f568ed2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1061 4334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "402d2fd6-c005-5674-be33-360f1b1762d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1061 4336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9871c72-8aa0-5596-948e-55a61792f893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with sums or differences of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same algebraic expression under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ This is similar to collecting like terms in a linear expression.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{x}-3\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (5-3)\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1065 5971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "deda60a8-4b35-50ac-a71c-d3f884aa8e1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative.\n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1065 6012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "30549c94-702e-5e00-8cdc-4a050a34243f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]x - 2\\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{x}-2\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ (4-2)\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-1} + \\sqrt[3]{x+1}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms, even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt x + \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt[3] x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1065 6013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a4a25921-e2bd-5bf7-99ed-3a8dd852fb70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1067 "Simplifying Expressions with Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501464 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501464 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501464 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00a3bbbb-3d58-5812-91fd-8458c0795915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501468 #edb/ref 1080 "5{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501468 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501468 #edb/ref 1083 